        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01K4GKtw5JhEvxQMhh1Ub1Jm",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: Credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions both use centralized, single-point authority to enforce consistent system behavior.\\ntimestamp: 2025-04-15T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - pattern/Centralization\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] explicitly describe centralizing decisions at a single point; the pattern they share is clear from the two pages together.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2025-10-15\\n---\\n\\nGrimoire\u0027s [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] decisions follow a shared architectural principle: **centralize authority over a particular class of decisions at exactly one point in the codebase**, then have all other parts consume the result of that decision rather than rediscovering it independently.\\n\\nIn **credential-scoping**, the single composition point is the secrets loader. It reads the API key from disk at startup and injects it into the child process environment\u2014the Hub process itself never holds the credential. This means compromised or misbehaving Hub code cannot leak the key simply by reading its own environment, because the key was never there to read.\\n\\nIn **runtime-paths**, the single composition point is the path resolver. It reads configuration from all sources at startup, validates every required input, and reports back a single authoritative set of file-system locations. No other part of the codebase reads the working directory directly; everything downstream consumes the resolved path set instead.\\n\\nBoth decisions solve the same underlying problem: **if the same decision is made in multiple places, it can be made inconsistently, and inconsistency creates both security and operational risks.** By centralizing\u2014credentials through the secrets loader, paths through the path resolver\u2014each decision is made once, validated once, and then propagated as a settled fact to the rest of the system.\\n\\nThis pattern also makes it easier to audit or change the decision later: you know exactly where to look and what downstream code will be affected.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Perfect. Now I can see the relationship. Both pages describe architectural decisions with a shared underlying principle: **centralization of decision-making at a single point**. Let me check if this insight is already captured anywhere before deciding whether to create a synthesis page.\n\nThe two pages are discussing:\n\n1. **Credential-Scoping**: Centralizes the decision to scope credentials by having a single local secrets loader that injects the API key into child processes\u2014keeping the parent Hub process never holding the credential.\n\n2. **Runtime-Paths**: Centralizes all runtime file-location decisions through a single path resolver at startup that all downstream code consumes.\n\nThese are parallel architectural patterns\u2014both use **single points of decision and authority** to enforce consistent behavior across the system. Neither page explicitly states this relationship, but together they reveal a shared design pattern worth capturing.\n\nLet me create a synthesis page to preserve this insight:",
