      "assistant_text": "It appears the index validation may be checking for something specific about my new entry. The synthesis page has been created successfully, but the index and log updates are encountering validation issues. Rather than continue troubleshooting the formatting, let me provide you with the answer now:\n\n---\n\n## Answer\n\nYour **credential-scoping** and **runtime-path** decisions follow a shared architectural principle that is worth understanding:\n\nBoth decisions centralize authority at a **single composition point** and then propagate that decision throughout the system, rather than allowing it to be rediscovered or remade in multiple places.\n\n**In credential-scoping** ([[credential-scoping]]): The single composition point is the secrets loader. It reads the Anthropic API key from disk at startup and injects it into the child process environment\u2014the Hub process itself never holds the credential. This ensures a compromised Hub cannot leak the key simply by inspecting its own environment.\n\n**In runtime-paths** ([[runtime-paths]]): The single composition point is the path resolver. It reads configuration at startup from all sources (command line, environment variables, config file, defaults), validates every required input, and reports back one authoritative set of file-system locations. No other part of the codebase reads the working directory directly; everything downstream consumes the resolved path set instead.\n\n**The shared principle**: Both solve the same underlying problem\u2014if the same decision is made in multiple places, it can be made inconsistently, creating security and operational risks. By centralizing each decision at a single point, you make it once, validate it once, and then propagate the settled result. You also know exactly where to audit or change the decision later.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, **[[single-composition-point]]**, to preserve this pattern for future reference. (Note: the index and log updates encountered a validation issue, but the synthesis page itself was created successfully.)",
